<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ayanis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ayanis is the site of a Urartian fortress on the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusa-ii">Rusa II</a> in the seventh century BCE and known in antiquity as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toprakkale">Rusahinili</a> Eiduru-kai. Excavations since 1989 have uncovered a temple of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haldi">Haldi</a> with inlaid stone walls, storerooms of large jars and many inscriptions. The fortress was destroyed by fire later in the seventh century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ayanis.jpg" alt="Ayanis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clemens Schmillen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_Museum_-_Inscription_Ayanis_Fortress.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
